  "strategy": "Numbered plan for multi-scale optimization to improve Erd\u0151s minimum overlap constant:\n\n<user-to-coder>Implementation plan:</user-to-coder>\n\n1. <b>Problem formulation and justification</b>\n   - Goal: Minimize maximum overlap integral for step functions on [0,2] with integral 1\n   - Key idea: Use multi-scale approach to escape local minima by systematically optimizing at multiple resolutions\n   - Rationale: Hierarchical optimization bridges between fine-grained local search and coarse-grained global exploration\n\n2. <b>Mathematical specification</b>\n   - Representation: Step functions h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with sum(h)=n/2 (adjust n_points as needed)\n   - Objective: Compute C_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Projection between grids: \n       Down-projection: Average k=\u230an/m\u230b consecutive points, then scale to satisfy integral=1\n       Up-projection: Interpolate coarse grid using linear interpolation preserving integral\n   - Optimization formulation: Use L-BFGS-B with integral constraint projected via penalty term (parameterized by \u03b1)\n\n3. <b>Implementation procedure</b>\n   <steps>\n   <step>01. Setup projection functions</step>\n   ```python\n   def project_down(x, m, n):\n       k = n // m\n       y_coarse = np.zeros(m)\n       for i in range(m):\n           start_idx = i*k\n           end_idx = start_idx+k\n           if end_idx>=n: \n               return None  # Invalid when grid size mismatches\n           y_coarse[i] = np.mean(x[start_idx:end_idx])\n       y_filled = project_feasible(y_coarse, m/2.0)\n       return y_filled\n    \n   def project_up(y_parent, m, n):\n       k = n // m\n       y_child = np.zeros(n)\n       for i in range(m):\n           start_idx = i*k\n           end_idx = start_idx+k\n           if end_idx>n:\n               end_idx = n\n           y_child[start_idx:end_idx] = np.mean(y_parent[i] * np.ones(k), dtype=float)\n       return y_child\n   ```\n   \n   <step>02. Multi-grid optimization loop</step>\n   ```python\n   def optimize_grid(h_start, target_sum, n):\n       n_length = len(h_start)\n       dx = 2.0/n_length\n       penalty=5000.0\n       best_h = project_feasible(h_start, target_sum)\n       best_score = compute_c5(best_h)\n   \n       # Optimization settings\n       alphasp = [5, 15, 40, 100, 250]\n       topksp = [40, 40, 40, 40, 40]\n       maxitersp = [30, 40, 80, 80, 80]\n       ftolsp = [1e-12, 1e-12, 1e-12, 1e-12, 1e-12]\n   \n       for i, alpha in enumerate(alphas_phase1):\n           h_new = lbfgs_step(h_start, target_sum, alpha, topksp[i], penalty, maxitersp[i], bounds_pen, ftolsp[i])\n           #... [Standard lbfgs_step implementation omitted for brevity]...\n   \n       return best_h, best_score\n   ```\n   \n   <step>03. Main optimization strategy</step>\n   ```python\n   def run(seed=42, budget_s=1300, **kwargs):\n       start_time = time.time()\n       def time_left():\n           return budget_s - 50 - (time.time() - start_time)\n   \n       # Grid hierarchy (must be divisors of each other)\n       grid_sizes = [400, 200, 100, 50, 40, 20, 10, 5]\n       n0 = 400\n       best_candidate = None\n       best_score = None\n       allocation_per_grid = [450, 360, 270, 220, 190, lookup grid sizes]\n       current_time = 50  # for initialization\n   \n       # 1. Coarsest grid optimization\n       for i, n_opt in enumerate(grid_sizes):\n           if current_time > allocation_per_grid[i] or time_left() < 60:\n               break   # Safe early exit\n   \n           # Project from coarser grid if exists\n           if i <= len(grid_sizes)-2:\n               h_proj = project_down(best_candidate, n_opt, grid_sizes[i+1]) if best_candidate is not None else None\n   \n           # Optimization\n           h0 = initial_h_values if n_opt==n0 else (ran_dom_candidate if h_proj is None else h_proj)\n           best_candidate_grid, best_score_grid = optimize_grid(h0, n_opt/2, n_opt)\n   \n           # Track best candidate\n           if best_c. and best_score_grid < best_score or best_candidate is None:\n               best_candidate = best_candidate_grid\n               best_score = best_score_grid\n   \n           # Update time tracking\n           current_time += allocation_per_grid[i]\n   \n       # Final projection to parent resolution\n       if best_candidate is not None:\n           best_candidate = project_up(best_candidate, n0, len(best_candidate))\n   \n       return best_candidate, best_score, len(best_candidate)\n   ```\n\n4. <b>Search choices</b>\n   - Starting grids: Start from coarsest grid (n=5) upward to find global feasibility\n   - Projection strategy:\n     \u2022 Down-projection: Maintain integral via averaging\n     \u2022 Up-projection: Preserve integral through careful interpolation\n   - Termination: Allocate time proportional to grid size (inversely)\n   - Key parameters:\n     \u2022 Global: \u03b1\u2208[5,500], penalty=5000\n     \u2022 Local: timesteps proportional to computing resources\n\n5. <b>Computation budget</b>\n   - Grid time allocations: 450s (400), 360s (200), 270s (100), 220s (50)\n   - Total time: Critical timing for arrival\n   \u03a3 allocations_per_grid = 1300s\n   - Fast abort: Check at start of each grid optimization\n\n6. <b>Validation contract</b>\n   - Feasibility check: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)=target_sum (via project_feasible)\n   - Performance: Compute final score with compute_c5 and include time_left() sampling in start=50s\n   - Return contract: Three-element tuple (h_values, score, n_points) with exact integer n_points\n\n<b>Key mathematical derivation:</b>\nThe method ensures that low-overlap solutions are transferred between grids via projection while maintaining the integral constraint. For a function optimized at grid size n\u2081 projected to n\u2082:\n\n\u222bh(x) dx = n\u2082/2 (by our projection construction)\n\nwhile:\n\nOverlap integral is bounded by the grid-averaged correlation structure.\n\n<b>Technical requirements:</b>\nImplemented using numpy, scipy.optimize (L-BFGS-B), and custom projection functions. Requires validation that projections maintain the core problem structure, but this is handled by our grid sequencing strategy.",
